  "content": {
    "title": "Women's Selection Show: Time, TV, UConn's NCAA seed",
    "text": "UNCASVILLE, CONNECTICUT - MARCH 09: Sarah Strong #21 of the Connecticut Huskies lines up before the championship game of the Big East Women’s Basketball Tournament against the Villanova Wildcats at Mohegan Sun Arena.Joe Buglewicz/Getty ImagesUConn women's basketball and Fairfield learned their NCAA Tournament fates Sunday evening, both heading to Regional 1 - Fort Worth. Despite a late push for UCLA to be considered, UConn earned the tournament's No. 1 overall seed, rewarded by the Selection Committee for a remarkable undefeated regular season. Meanwhile, Fairfield earned its highest NCAA Tournament seed since 1988, as the No. 11-seeded Stags will take on Notre Dame in hopes of achieving the program's first-ever March Madness win. Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢ Bracket is set All 68 teams have been announced in the NCAA Tournament. Check out the complete bracket below: South Carolina earns final No. 1 seed The Gamecocks are back to the No. 1 line after another excellent season under Dawn Staley. Article continues below this ad Rhode Island earns No. 11 seed UConn's neighbors are dancing, as Rhode Island earned a No. 11 seed in the 2026 NCAA Tournament. The Rams are heading to Regional 3 to face off against No. 6 seed Alabama. More For You Bracket halfway done Two more regions remain for the NCAA Tournament Selection Committee to reveal. Article continues below this ad Ex-Husky Carla Berube leads Princeton to No. 9 seed Carla Berube is back in the NCAA Tournament with Princeton, facing off against Oklahoma State in the 8-9 game. If Berube can advance past the Pokes, a massive challenge awaits in the Round of 32 with No. 1 seed UCLA looming. UConn's region is set Geno Auriemma is on a crash course toward facing his former player, Shea Ralpha, in the Elite Eight this year. Ralph is currently the coach of Vanderbilt, which was awarded the No. 2 seed in UConn's Regional 1 - Fort Worth. Elsewhere, the region includes some familiar opponents, with UConn having already beaten Ohio State and Notre Dame this season. Article continues below this ad Fairfield earns No. 11 seed The Stags have earned their highest-ever seed under coach Carly Thibault-DuDonis, selected as a No. 11 seed in Regional 1 - Fort Worth. Iowa State, Syracuse round out Storrs region Iowa State and Syracuse will meet up in the 8 vs. 9 matchup at Gampel Pavilion Saturday. The winner will face UConn in the Round of 32. Article continues below this ad UConn will face UTSA Earning the No. 1 overall seed, UConn will welcome No. 16 seed UTSA to Storrs Saturday. The Roadrunners finished the season 18-16. UConn earns No. 1 overall seed UConn has earned the No. 1 overall seed in the women's NCAA Tournament. Despite some speculation that UCLA may take that honor, the Huskies were rewarded for an undefeated regular season.Article continues below this ad Rebecca Lobo: 'Last dance' Rebecca Lobo gave her opening thoughts on ESPN's Women’s Basketball Selection Special Sunday evening, stating that the tournament feels like a \"last dance\" for many players that the college basketball world has watched grow up in recent years. She listed UConn's Azzi Fudd among these players. Selection show is live! Will UConn earn a No. 1 overall seed? That question will be answered over the next hour, starting nowArticle continues below this ad 30 minutes until Selection Show The NCAA Women’s Basketball Selection Special will begin airing on ESPN in 30 minutes. Former UConn players lead teams to NCAA Tournament Geno Auriemma will be joined by a pair his former players this year in the NCAA Tournament, with both Shea Ralph and Carla Berube poised to make strong runs in March this year.Article continues below this adCT Insider LogoMake CT Insider a preferred source on Google to see more of our journalism when you search.Add Preferred SourceRalph has formed a bona fide contender to Auriemma for the national title, winning 27 games in a loaded SEC conference and currently standing as the No. 6 team in Monday's AP Poll. Meanwhile, Carla Berube secured an automatic berth Saturday afternoon after leading Princeton to the Ivy League Tournament title, with the Tigers projected as a No. 10 seed by ESPN bracketologist Charlie Creme. What a CT bracketologist says about UConn, Fairfield Megan Gauer, a HerHoopStats bracketologist, believes that UConn will hold onto the No. 1 overall seed despite a mounting campaign for UCLA. She also believes that Fairfield could earn its first NCAA Tournament win with the right matchup. Article continues below this adUConn: \"I know that there are a lot of people, and it's like an Internet thing, that it should be UCLA. I understand the argument. They have 18 Quad-1 wins and they rolled through the Big Ten. But I don't think much has changed in the last two weeks, and we saw the committee in its reveals hold strong with UConn being the overall No. 1. I expect them to stick with that decision Sunday.\"Fairfield: \"What Fairfield is going to do in the tournament is so matchup dependent,\" Gauer said. \"If they get a matchup against someone with not a lot of size they could upset someone. Right now I have them with Baylor and Baylor has some size, but I wouldn't be surprised if that's a tight game. But a team like N.C. State would be a horrible matchup for them. Notre Dame would be a great matchup for Fairfield. I'm interested to see what region they go to.\" How to watch women's Selection Show The NCAA Women’s Basketball Selection Special will be aired on ESPN at 8 p.m. ET. Former UConn center Rebecca Lobo will be among the studio hosts for Sunday's broadcast. Article continues below this ad Muffet McGraw: UCLA over UConn for No. 1 overall seed While UConn held a unanimous grip on the No. 1 spot in the AP Poll for much of the season, there has been a growing contingent of fans endorsing UCLA as the No. 1 overall seed in the NCAA Tournament, citing the Bruins' 19-9 edge in Quadrant 1 wins. Former Notre Dame coach supported this perspective on X over the weekend, adding to her frosty relationship with Geno Auriemma and the UConn program. \"So if Princeton had gone undefeated in the regular season, would they be a No. 1 seed?\" McGraw wrote on X Saturday. \"Or would they be penalized for playing in the Ivy League? The seeds should be a reward for how you played during the regular season, and UCLA had more success against a tougher schedule than UConn.\" Fairfield hoping for NCAA Tournament breakthrough The Stags have now made three consecutive NCAA Tournaments under coach Carly Thibault-DuDonis, but Fairfield is still looking to secure its first win in March Madness. With her Stags hoping to earn a No. 10 seed, Thibault-DuDonis is eyeing a favorable matchup to pick up this breakthrough victory.Article continues below this ad\"I worry less about the seed, a 10 or 11, than about the matchup,\" Thibault-DuDonis said. \"That makes the difference in our ability to go and make a run. The seed is what it is but in the last two years we've been given tough matchups against teams that deserved to be seeded higher.\"CT Insider LogoGet daily updates from the UConn basketball world.March 15, 2026|Updated March 15, 2026 10:06 p.m.Trending Reporter/Audience ProducerDeclan Walsh is a Trending Reporter and Audience Producer at Hearst Connecticut, focusing on trending topics, lifestyle and sports. Originally from New Jersey, Declan studied Journalism-Sports Media at the University of Florida. He previously covered women's basketball at NCAA.com and made stops at Sports Illustrated, FOX Sports and The Palm Beach Post.",
